{
  "name": "Log email receipts to Google Sheets with OpenAI and send monthly summaries",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "f3a0s001-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
      "name": "Sticky Note — read me first",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -840,
        -180
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 720,
        "height": 1100,
        "content": "## Email receipts in, clean expense ledger out\n\n### How it works\nForward any receipt to your own inbox with \"receipt\" in the subject (or point the Gmail filter at a label). The workflow takes the attachment — a text PDF or a JPG/PNG photo — reads it with AI, and appends one row to your Google Sheets ledger: date, merchant, amount, currency and a category picked from **your** list. The ledger itself is the guard: an email that is already logged is never processed twice. On the 1st of each month (8am, your n8n timezone) it emails you last month's spending by category. A receipt it cannot read (scanned PDF without text, HEIC photo) is not logged — you get an email saying so instead.\n\nThis organizes records — it is **not tax advice**; review categories and amounts with your accountant.\n\n### Setup\n1. Connect **Gmail** and **Google Sheets** (one-click sign-in on n8n Cloud).\n2. Add your **OpenAI API key** to both AI nodes.\n3. Create a sheet with a tab named exactly `receipts` and headers: `processed_at, gmail_message_id, receipt_date, merchant, amount, currency, category, source_subject`.\n4. In **⚙️ Your settings** paste the sheet URL into **sheetUrl**, set your email, default currency and category list. That is the only place you name the sheet — never pick one inside a Sheets node.\n5. Open every node that uses a credential once (Gmail trigger, three Sheets nodes, two Gmail send nodes, two OpenAI nodes) and check the credential is attached; leave URL and tab fields as they are. n8n will not activate a workflow with an unattached credential.\n6. Activate, then forward yourself a receipt with \"receipt\" in the subject.\n\n### Customization tips\nChange the Gmail filter (e.g. `label:receipts`). Edit the category list any time — the AI only picks from it."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "f3a0s002-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
      "name": "Sticky Note — section 1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -80,
        -180
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 1320,
        "height": 1100,
        "content": "## 1. Catch + guard\nTwo triggers share one settings node: receipts arrive by email, the monthly summary fires on the 1st. Before anything is read, the ledger is loaded once and compared in code — an email that is already in the ledger is never processed twice, and several receipts arriving together are each handled on their own."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "f3a0s003-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
      "name": "Sticky Note — section 2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1260,
        -180
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 1340,
        "height": 1100,
        "content": "## 2. Read the receipt\nThe receipt file is the PDF attachment if there is one, otherwise the largest image (so an email logo is not mistaken for the receipt). PDFs get their text extracted directly; photos go through AI vision transcription. Both paths converge into one structured extraction that returns date, merchant, amount, currency and a category **only from your list**. Anything that cannot be read (a scanned PDF with no text, a HEIC photo, no attachment) is not logged — instead you get an email saying so."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "f3a0s004-0000-4000-8000-000000000004",
      "name": "Sticky Note — section 3",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        2620,
        -180
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 520,
        "height": 1100,
        "content": "## 3. Ledger + monthly summary\nEach receipt becomes one row in Google Sheets. On the 1st of the month, last month's rows are aggregated by category and emailed to you — so you start every month knowing where the money went. (Not tax advice.)"
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
